bio='''<section class="section profile" id="about"><div><p class="kicker">About David</p><h2>Close to the work.</h2></div><div><p>I'm a Lead Software Engineer in Fort Worth, Texas. I work on healthcare data platforms, from event-driven ingestion on AWS to PostgreSQL warehouses. I care about understanding failure, making recovery repeatable, and explaining the decisions behind the system.</p><p style="margin-top:20px">Outside software, I cook, play video games, and spend probably too much time watching educational YouTube. I like debugging alongside people and talking through the reasoning.</p><a class="action" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/david-huson">Connect on LinkedIn ↗</a></div></section>'''
homes={
'a':'''<section class="journal-hero"><div class="folio">David Huson<span>Lead software engineer<br>Fort Worth, Texas</span></div><div><p class="kicker">Software / Data / Systems</p><h1>Software, with a clear line of reasoning.</h1><p class="lead">I build healthcare data platforms. This is a record of the systems, decisions, and tradeoffs behind my work.</p><a class="action" href="#work">Explore the work ↓</a></div></section><section class="section" id="work"><div class="sectionhead"><p class="kicker">01 / Selected work</p><p>Start with a decision. Follow it into the implementation.</p></div><article class="journal-feature"><div><p class="meta">Earlier work · Language design · C</p><h2 style="margin-top:20px">When the grammar becomes the program.</h2><p class="lead" style="margin-top:22px;font-size:18px">A small compiler front end. One function per grammar rule, with explicit choices about memory, errors and scope.</p><a class="action" href="a-case.html">Read the case study ↗</a></div><figure class="plate"><p class="meta">Figure 01 / Parser stages</p>FLOW<figcaption class="note">A simplified view of the documented coursework pipeline.</figcaption></figure></article><div class="journal-list"><article><span class="proposal">Proposed professional story</span><h3>Making recovery repeatable.</h3><p>Event-driven healthcare ingestion, idempotent loads and the boundaries that make replay possible.</p></article><article><span class="proposal">Proposed professional story</span><h3>A warehouse sized to its work.</h3><p>PostgreSQL, dimensional models and the cost of choosing more platform than a workload needs.</p></article></div></section>''',
'b':'''<section class="atlas-hero"><div><p class="kicker">David Huson / Lead software engineer</p><h1>Understand the system.<br>Then change it.</h1></div><p class="lead">I build healthcare data platforms in Fort Worth, Texas. I work across ingestion, service boundaries and analytical models, with recovery designed into the flow.</p></section><section class="atlas-map" aria-label="Featured parser architecture"><div class="map-top"><p class="meta">System 01 / Language processing</p><span class="meta">Earlier work · C</span></div>FLOW<div class="atlas-bottom"><div><p class="meta">Decision boundary</p><a class="action" href="b-case.html">Inspect this system ↗</a></div><div><h3>One grammar rule. One function.</h3><p style="margin-top:12px">A recursive descent parser makes the language structure visible in the implementation. The cost is a parser coupled to a fixed grammar.</p><details><summary>Why this boundary matters</summary><p>Precedence follows from the nesting of expression, term and factor. Errors terminate through one match function, keeping diagnostics precise at the cost of reporting one error per run.</p></details></div></div></section><section id="work" class="atlas-rows"><div class="sectionhead"><h2>Systems & decisions</h2><p>Technical scope, followed by the reasoning.</p></div><article><span class="meta">01</span><div><span class="proposal">Proposed professional story</span><h3>Recoverable ingestion</h3></div><p>AWS queues separate stages. Idempotent loads make retries safe. The professional case study needs a confirmed topology and incident account.</p></article><article><span class="meta">02</span><div><span class="proposal">Proposed professional story</span><h3>Analytical data models</h3></div><p>PostgreSQL and Supabase with Kimball star schemas. The story should explain workload sizing and the point at which the decision changes.</p></article></section>''',
'c':'''<section class="quiet-hero"><div><p class="kicker">David Huson · Software engineer</p><h1>Care in the decisions.<br>Clarity in the system.</h1><p class="lead">I build healthcare data platforms, and spend much of my time making complicated processes easier to understand and recover.</p><a class="action" href="#work">Selected work ↓</a></div><figure class="quiet-photo"><img src="../../public/assets/portrait.webp" width="480" height="620" alt="David Huson outdoors, with sunglasses on his head"><figcaption>David Huson<br>Lead Software Engineer<br>Fort Worth, Texas</figcaption></figure></section><section class="section" id="work"><div class="sectionhead"><h2>A few considered decisions.</h2><p>Selected work and the questions behind it.</p></div><div class="quiet-list"><article><span class="meta">01 / C</span><div><p class="meta" style="margin:0 0 14px">Earlier work</p><h3><a href="c-case.html">How much parser does a small language need?</a></h3><p>Recursive descent, deliberate constraints, and a preference for errors that can be explained.</p><a class="action" href="c-case.html">Read the study ↗</a></div></article><article><span class="meta">02</span><div><span class="proposal">Proposed professional story</span><h3>Recovering without reconstructing.</h3><p>Designing healthcare ingestion around repeatable loads and clear failure boundaries.</p></div></article><article><span class="meta">03</span><div><span class="proposal">Proposed professional story</span><h3>The case for a smaller warehouse.</h3><p>Letting the workload determine the platform, and knowing when to reconsider.</p></div></article></div></section>''',
'd':'''<section class="open-hero"><p class="kicker">David Huson / Lead software engineer / Fort Worth</p><h1 class="headline">Make it work.<span>Make it clear.</span></h1><div class="open-intro"><p class="meta">Software engineering<br>Data infrastructure<br>System design</p><p class="lead">I build healthcare data platforms. I want the architecture, the failure modes, and the reasons behind each decision to be understandable.</p><a class="action" href="#work">Enter the work ↓</a></div></section><section id="work"><article class="open-band feature"><div><p class="open-number">01</p><p class="meta">Earlier work<br>Language design / C</p></div><div><h2>A small language.<br>An explicit structure.</h2><p>The parser's functions follow the grammar. Its tradeoffs are just as visible.</p>FLOW<a class="action" href="d-case.html">Follow the decisions ↗</a></div></article><article class="open-band"><div class="open-number">02</div><div><span class="proposal">Proposed professional story</span><h2>Failure is part<br>of the system.</h2><p>Event-driven ingestion and the design of recoverable data flows.</p></div></article><article class="open-band"><div class="open-number">03</div><div><span class="proposal">Proposed professional story</span><h2>Fit the platform<br>to the problem.</h2><p>Dimensional modeling on PostgreSQL and Supabase.</p></div></article></section>'''}
case='''<header class="article-head"><a href="KEY-home.html#work">← Selected work</a><p class="kicker">Case study / Earlier work / Coursework</p><h1>A small language.<br>A readable parser.</h1><p class="lead">Building a compiler front end in C, with a direct relationship between the grammar and the code that recognizes it.</p><div class="article-meta"><span>Role · Course project author</span><span>Language · C</span><span>Scope · Lexer, parser, IR</span></div></header><div class="article-layout"><nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#context">01 Context</a><a href="#architecture">02 Architecture</a><a href="#decisions">03 Decisions</a><a href="#outcome">04 Outcome</a></nav><article class="article-body"><section id="context"><p class="kicker">01 / Context & constraints</p><h2>Make the language rules executable.</h2><p>For a programming languages course, I built a front end that tokenizes input, checks its grammar, tracks identifiers and reports syntax errors. A follow-on part emitted an intermediate representation with simulated register allocation.</p><p>The implementation had to be C. That meant allocating identifier buffers, managing their lifetime and implementing a symbol table without the C++ standard library.</p></section><section id="architecture"><p class="kicker">02 / Architecture</p><h2>The call structure follows the grammar.</h2><figure class="diagram">FLOW<figcaption>Simplified from the published project account. Symbol-table lookup supports identifier handling. The IR stage belongs to the follow-on project.</figcaption></figure><p>Each nonterminal has a parser function. Expressions call terms, terms call factors, and parenthesized factors re-enter expression parsing. That nesting gives multiplication precedence over addition.</p><pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Grammar excerpt"><code>expression → term { ('+' | '-') term }
term       → factor { ('*' | '/') factor }
factor     → identifier | number
           | '(' expression ')'</code></pre><details><summary>Inspect symbol-table responsibility</summary><p>A hand-written hash table uses buckets and chaining for collisions. Lookup happens at each identifier reference. Explicit allocation and cleanup are part of the implementation, not services provided by the language runtime.</p></details></section><section id="decisions"><p class="kicker">03 / Decisions & tradeoffs</p><h2>One error, precisely located.</h2><p>All syntax-error reporting funnels through the match function. On a mismatch it reports the expected token, line and column, frees the symbol table and exits.</p><p class="tradeoff">The cost is one error per run. Panic-mode recovery could report more errors, but would also require synchronization rules and care to avoid misleading follow-on diagnostics.</p><details><summary>Why recursive descent?</summary><p>One function per nonterminal is straightforward to read and debug for this small grammar. It cannot directly handle left recursion, and changing the language means changing C source. Those limits were acceptable for fixed coursework scope.</p></details><details><summary>Why a hash table?</summary><p>Identifiers are looked up repeatedly. A chained hash table avoids a full list scan for every lookup, at the cost of managing buffers, buckets and collision chains explicitly.</p></details></section><section id="outcome"><p class="kicker">04 / Outcome & limits</p><h2>A working front end, with a clear boundary.</h2><p>The lexer and parser handle comments and multiline statements, track identifiers and report syntax errors with line and column. The second part emits three-address statements over virtual registers.</p><p>This is coursework, not a production compiler. There is no optimization pass and register allocation does not target real hardware. A larger, changing language would justify revisiting the parsing strategy.</p><a class="action" href="https://github.com/David-Huson/COP4020-ProgrammingLanguages/tree/main/project1">Inspect the project source ↗</a></section></article></div>'''
for key,name,kind,bg,surface,ink,muted,accent,line,font in concepts:
    tokens=f':root{{--bg:{bg};--surface:{surface};--ink:{ink};--muted:{muted};--accent:{accent};--line:{line};--display:{font}}}'
    swatches=''.join(f'<div class="swatch"><b style="background:{value}"></b>{label}<br>{value}</div>' for label,value in [('Background',bg),('Surface',surface),('Text',ink),('Secondary',muted),('Accent',accent),('Border',line)])
    system=f'''<header class="system-title"><p class="kicker">{kind}</p><h1>{name}</h1><p>A working specimen. These are actual rendered fonts and tokens, not an image of an interface.</p></header><section class="section"><h2>Color</h2><div class="swatches" style="margin-top:32px">{swatches}</div><p class="note">Accent identifies links, focus and diagram boundaries. No decorative status colors.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Typography</h2><div class="specimen" style="margin-top:32px"><p class="meta">Display · {html.escape(font)} · 46–100px desktop / 44–58px mobile</p><h2 style="font-size:clamp(40px,5vw,72px);margin-top:18px">The decision is the story.</h2></div><div class="specimen"><p class="meta">Body · Avenir Next / Arial · 16–20px · line height 1.6</p><p class="lead" style="margin-top:20px">A queue separates when work arrives from when it can be processed. That boundary is useful only if the consumer can recover safely.</p></div><div class="specimen"><p class="meta">Annotations · SFMono / Consolas · 12px · uppercase tracking .07em</p><p class="note">System fonts keep these prototypes self-contained. A final font choice requires a Windows/Android fallback review.</p></div></section><section class="section"><h2>Interaction states</h2><div class="states"><a href="{key}-case.html">Default link ↗</a><a class="hover" href="{key}-case.html">Hover sample ↗</a><a class="focus" href="{key}-case.html">Focus sample ↗</a></div><details><summary>Closed disclosure. Open with Enter or a tap.</summary><p>Technical depth appears in the document flow. No modal, scroll lock or hover dependency. The control remains accessible without JavaScript.</p></details><details open><summary>Open disclosure</summary><p>The label remains in place and the explanation appears beneath it. The relationship between control and content stays clear.</p></details><p class="note">Navigate with Tab to inspect real focus. Use the Case study link to test navigation. Reduced-motion preferences disable smooth scrolling. No entry animation or delayed content.</p></section>'''
    for view,content in [('home',homes[key].replace('FLOW',flow)+bio),('case',case.replace('KEY',key).replace('FLOW',flow)),('system',system)]:
        document=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="robots" content="noindex,nofollow"><title>{name} · {view} · David Huson design study</title><style>{tokens}{base}{styles[key]}</style></head><body class="{key}"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><div class="review"><a href="index.html">← Four directions</a><span>{key.upper()} / {name} · New copy proposed</span><span><a href="{key}-case.html">Case study</a> · <a href="{key}-system.html">Design system</a></span></div><div class="wrap"><header class="nav"><a class="brand" href="{key}-home.html">David Huson</a><nav class="navlinks" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="{key}-home.html#work">Work</a><a href="{key}-home.html#about">About</a><a href="https://github.com/David-Huson">GitHub ↗</a></nav></header><main id="main">{content}</main><footer class="footer"><div>David Huson / Fort Worth, Texas</div><div><a href="https://github.com/David-Huson">GitHub ↗</a><a href="mailto:dhuson@davidhuson.dev">Email</a><a href="https://davidhuson.dev">Consulting ↗</a></div></footer></div></body></html>'''
        (P/f'{key}-{view}.html').write_text(document)
